#N canvas 100 100 1250 780 12;
#X obj 40 20 inlet~;
#X obj 620 20 loadbang;
#X msg 620 60 1;
#X obj 620 110 metro 50;
#X obj 620 150 t b b;
#X obj 300 60 env~ 1024;
#X obj 300 100 dbtorms;
#X obj 300 200 f;
#X obj 300 240 t f f;
#X obj 40 110 peakamp~;
#X obj 40 240 t f f;
#X obj 40 300 pack f f f f;
#X obj 40 340 list prepend meter \$1;
#X obj 40 380 list trim;
#X obj 40 700 outlet;
#N canvas 100 100 560 460 fft 0;
#X obj 30 20 inlet~;
#X obj 320 20 block~ 128 1;
#X obj 200 70 tabreceive~ \$0-hann;
#X obj 30 110 *~;
#X obj 30 160 rfft~;
#X obj 30 210 *~;
#X obj 160 210 *~;
#X obj 30 260 +~;
#X obj 30 300 sqrt~;
#X obj 30 340 *~ 0.015625;
#X obj 30 390 tabsend~ \$0-spx;
#X text 330 110 Hann window \, then a 128-point real FFT: 64 usable
magnitude bins \, which is exactly the 64 the panel draws. [block~ 128
1] reblocks this subpatch only -- the rest of the patch keeps running
at its normal block size., f 44;
#X text 330 260 sqrt(re^2 + im^2) \, scaled by 1/64 (N/2) so a full-scale
sine lands near 1.0. Sent LINEAR: Pd vanilla has no [log~] \, and the
panel rescales for its own plot height anyway \, so the dB conversion
lives in JS -- see gui_hub_bridge.js's TELEMETRY table., f 44;
#X connect 0 0 3 0;
#X connect 2 0 3 1;
#X connect 3 0 4 0;
#X connect 4 0 5 0;
#X connect 4 0 5 1;
#X connect 4 1 6 0;
#X connect 4 1 6 1;
#X connect 5 0 7 0;
#X connect 6 0 7 1;
#X connect 7 0 8 0;
#X connect 8 0 9 0;
#X connect 9 0 10 0;
#X restore 40 460 pd fft;
#X obj 880 110 metro 100;
#X obj 880 150 array get \$0-spx;
#X obj 880 190 list split 64;
#X obj 880 230 list prepend spectrum \$1;
#X obj 880 270 list trim;
#N canvas 0 0 300 200 (subpatch) 0;
#X array \$0-spx 128 float 0;
#A color 0;
#A width 1;
#X coords 0 1 128 -1 200 80 1 0 0;
#X restore 880 340 graph;
#N canvas 0 0 300 200 (subpatch) 0;
#X array \$0-hann 128 float 0;
#A color 0;
#A width 1;
#X coords 0 1 128 -1 200 80 1 0 0;
#X restore 880 470 graph;
#X msg 620 250 cosinesum 128 0.5 -0.5;
#X obj 620 300 s \$0-hann;
#X text 40 40 stem_telemetry~ <name>: a listen-only tap. Takes one mono
signal and reports what it is doing to bridge_guiHub \, which relays it
to the panel. It touches nothing -- no gain \, no routing \, no state --
so an instance can be dropped anywhere in the signal path without
changing a sample of what you hear. <name> is the stem label that goes
out in every message (vocals/melo/bass/drums \, or master on the sum)., f 68;
#X text 620 320 REPORT RATES. Meters at 20Hz \, spectrum at 10Hz. Both
are display rates \, not analysis rates -- the FFT itself runs every
128-sample block regardless \, and [tabsend~] just keeps overwriting
the array \, so what goes out is the most recent block rather than an
average. Deliberate: a meter that averages over 100ms hides exactly
the transients this instrument is built to chop up., f 68;
#X text 40 540 WHY L AND R CARRY THE SAME NUMBER. Every stem in this
patch is mono -- stem_timestretch~ has a single outlet~ \, and the
4-channel quad path the Max version metered was stripped during the
conversion (see CONVERSION_NOTES.md \, "4 channel quad meters"). The
message shape keeps the pair anyway \, because the master sum IS stereo
and because the panel and any later DAW-side build both expect L/R.
When per-stem stereo comes back \, feed inlet~ twice and split the
[t f f] pairs -- nothing downstream changes., f 68;
#X text 40 660 NOT LUFS. The RMS figure here is [env~] -> [dbtorms] \,
i.e. plain unweighted RMS. Real BS.1770 LUFS needs K-weighting (a
high-shelf plus a high-pass biquad \, coefficients that depend on the
sample rate) and gated integration \, none of which is here. It goes out
as "rmsdb" rather than "lufs" so nothing downstream can mistake it for
a calibrated loudness number. Adding true LUFS is two [biquad~] objects
and a gate -- real work \, but small \, and worth doing before anyone
uses this figure to make a mastering decision., f 68;
#X text 880 560 [array get] is the one object here not verified against
a running Pd -- it should output the whole array as a list on bang.
If it turns out to need an explicit onset/count \, that is a one-object
change. Flagged in CONVERSION_NOTES.md as the first thing to check on
a live smoke test., f 44;
